# 🎯 Support Vector Machines (SVM): Maximum Margin, Kernels & Practical ML

Today we'll learn one of the most important classical Machine Learning algorithms:

> **Support Vector Machine (SVM)** — an algorithm that finds the best boundary separating different classes by maximizing the margin between them.

This topic is extremely important for **ML interviews** because it combines **intuition, mathematics, optimization, and practical implementation**.

---

# 1. Executive Summary & Core Intuition

## What is SVM?

A **Support Vector Machine (SVM)** is a supervised machine learning algorithm primarily used for classification. It finds an optimal decision boundary, called a **hyperplane**, that separates classes while maximizing the distance (margin) between the boundary and the closest data points.

SVM can also solve **nonlinear problems** using the **kernel trick**.

### 🎤 Elevator Pitch

> SVM is a supervised learning algorithm that finds the optimal hyperplane separating classes by maximizing the margin between the decision boundary and the nearest training points, called support vectors.

---

## 🧠 ELI5 Mental Model

Imagine two groups of people:

```text
🔵 🔵 🔵 🔵

            🔴 🔴 🔴 🔴
```

You need to draw a line separating them.

There are many possible lines:

```text
Bad boundary ❌
🔵 🔵 🔵 │ 🔴 🔴 🔴
```

But SVM wants the boundary with the **largest safe distance** from both groups:

```text
🔵 🔵 🔵     │     🔴 🔴 🔴
             │
        Maximum Margin
```

The idea is:

> **Don't just separate the classes — separate them with the maximum possible safety margin.**

