# 21. Explain to an Interviewer — 60-Second Script 🎤

> A Support Vector Machine is a supervised learning algorithm that finds an optimal decision boundary, called a hyperplane, by maximizing the margin between different classes. The training samples closest to the boundary are called support vectors, and they are particularly important because they influence the position of the boundary. For data that is not linearly separable, SVM can use kernel functions, such as the RBF kernel, to model nonlinear decision boundaries. Important hyperparameters include C, which controls the penalty for classification errors, and gamma, which controls the locality of influence in kernels such as RBF. SVM is effective in high-dimensional spaces but can become computationally expensive for very large datasets and usually requires feature scaling.

---

# 22. Top 3 Interview Questions 🎯

## Q1. What are Support Vectors?

> Support vectors are the training samples closest to the decision boundary. They are critical because they influence the position and margin of the optimal separating hyperplane.

---

## Q2. What is the Kernel Trick?

> The kernel trick allows SVM to model nonlinear relationships by computing inner products in an implicitly transformed higher-dimensional feature space without explicitly calculating the transformation coordinates.

---

## Q3. What is the difference between C and Gamma?

> C controls how strongly the model penalizes classification errors versus maintaining a wider margin. Gamma, mainly for kernels such as RBF, controls how locally individual training samples influence the decision boundary. High gamma produces more localized and potentially complex boundaries.

